WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.010
There is an orange in your hand.

2
00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:11.140
You split it into five pieces; you do not stretch, crush or multiply any of them — you only rotate them and swap their places.

3
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:16.870
In the end two oranges, exact copies of each other, are sitting on the table.

4
00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:21.960
This is not a trick, but a theorem proven in nineteen twenty-four.

5
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.010
There's an orange sitting on the table.

6
00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.300
Its weight in your palm, the pores of its peel under your fingertips.

7
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.670
It even has a scent.

8
00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:45.240
Now suppose I tell you this: I can separate this orange into five pieces.

9
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:57.050
Without stretching the pieces, without straining them, without enlarging any of them, without adding any new matter to any of them, I will simply turn them in the air, slide them around, and put them back together.

10
00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:01.020
And when the work is done, there will be two oranges on the table.

11
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.510
Both of them exactly the size of the one we started with.

12
00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.840
Both of them solid, poreless, complete.

13
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:13.090
Everyone who hears this has the same first reaction: there's a trick in here somewhere.

14
00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:20.900
Either the pieces are stretching, or invisible gaps are slipping in between them, or the person speaking has a second orange hidden in their pocket.

15
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:26.150
Because this claim collides with one of the most basic things you have learned in your entire life.

16
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:32.040
When you cut something apart and put it back together, you are left with as much as you started with.

17
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.650
Cut a cake into eight slices and the cake is still exactly as much cake.

18
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.860
Volume is conserved.

19
00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:45.550
This isn't a belief, it's an observation; and that observation has not misled you even once, ever.

20
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:49.520
But what I just described is not a conjuring trick.

21
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.730
It's a theorem.

22
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:58.580
In nineteen twenty-four, two Polish mathematicians, Stefan Banach and Alfred Tarski, proved it in a joint paper.

23
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:04.310
In the world of mathematics the result is known by their names: the Banach-Tarski paradox.

24
00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:10.360
Pay attention to the word "paradox" here, because it invites misunderstanding.

25
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:18.650
In mathematics, a paradox does not mean "there is a contradiction in the system." It means "the result is true,

26
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:26.140
but your intuition cannot bear it." And the difference between those two sits at the heart of everything I am about to tell you.

27
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:33.790
First let's make the rules of the game clear, because until the rules are clear the claim looks either like magic or like nonsense.

28
00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:38.720
For a mathematician, a ball is not an object made of paper or of orange.

29
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.250
It's a set of points.

30
00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:46.660
The totality of all points whose distance from the center is less than some given number.

31
00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.670
A solid sphere.

32
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:56.200
It contains infinitely many points; so many that even trying to count that infinity would be meaningless.

33
00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:04.170
"Cutting into pieces," then, means this: you divide this set of points into subsets that never overlap one another.

34
00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:11.020
Every point goes into exactly one piece, none of them sits in two pieces at once, none of them is left out.

35
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:19.790
Don't imagine a knife; think instead of picking up every single point inside the ball, one by one, and placing each one into one of five separate boxes.

36
00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.960
"Moving" is the strictest rule of all.

37
00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.410
The only thing permitted is rigid motion: translation and rotation.

38
00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:33.140
You take the piece as a whole and carry it somewhere else, or you turn it about an axis.

39
00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:38.550
No stretching, no squeezing, no rescaling; not even mirroring is needed.

40
00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:43.960
The distance between any two points inside a piece remains exactly the same after the motion.

41
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.410
This is nothing more than sliding a cup from one end of the table to the other.

42
00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:59.260
And here is what the theorem says: you can divide a solid sphere into finitely many pieces and, using only these two kinds of motion, obtain two spheres, each an exact twin of the first.

43
00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:04.190
And when we say "finitely many," we really are talking about small numbers.

44
00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:10.240
In nineteen forty-seven Raphael Robinson showed that the job can be done with five pieces.

45
00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.050
What's more, he proved that you cannot go below five.

46
00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:19.300
If you leave the single point at the center out of the account, four pieces are enough.

47
00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:24.870
So this is not some trick that requires millions of pieces, that runs away to infinity.

48
00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:28.680
As many pieces as the fingers on one hand.

49
00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.010
Now let's ask the uncomfortable question.

50
00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:37.260
If the pieces do not stretch, then the volume of each piece must stay the same after the motion.

51
00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.030
Add up the volumes of the five pieces and you should get the volume of one ball.

52
00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:48.560
But those same five pieces, once they have changed places, give the volume of two balls.

53
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.010
A number cannot be equal both to itself and to twice itself.

54
00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.180
Somewhere in here, something is wrong.

55
00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:59.670
And the thing that is wrong is not where you would guess.

56
00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.520
The motions are flawless.

57
00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.210
The number of pieces is finite.

58
00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:07.740
The sphere really is a sphere.

59
00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:16.990
The assumption that collapses is hidden in that small word we used while building the sentence without ever noticing it: "volume." Those five pieces have no volume.

60
00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:21.760
Not zero, not very small, not too complicated to be measured.

61
00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.250
No number called volume can be assigned to them at all.

62
00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:30.500
And because there is nothing to add up, no inconsistency arises in the total either.

63
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:35.750
The idea of a body with no volume sounds like word play the first time you hear it.

64
00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:43.080
But it is a very concrete obstacle that mathematics discovered by stumbling into it at the beginning of the twentieth century.

65
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.770
The story begins with an ambition.

66
00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:52.620
At the end of the nineteenth century mathematicians wanted to set length, area and volume on solid foundations.

67
00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:58.670
The aim was bold: to find a rule that assigns a volume number to every subset of space.

68
00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:02.320
That rule had to satisfy three reasonable demands.

69
00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.290
The volume of a cube should come out the way we know it.

70
00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:09.940
Moving a body should not change its volume.

71
00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:14.870
And the volumes of two disjoint pieces should add up to the volume of their union.

72
00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:25.560
The measure theory developed by Henri Lebesgue did this with astonishing success; today that theory lies beneath integral calculus, beneath probability theory, beneath half of physics.

73
00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.330
But in nineteen oh five Giuseppe Vitali revealed the limit of that ambition.

74
00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:37.020
On the number line he constructed a set such that assigning any length whatsoever to it led directly to a contradiction.

75
00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:42.270
Saying zero didn't work, and saying a positive number didn't work either.

76
00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:46.080
The set lay outside the reach of the concept of length.

77
00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.890
Sets like these came to be called non-measurable sets.

78
00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:58.180
And from that day on, mathematicians had to accept the following: volume is not a property defined for everything.

79
00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:02.150
It is defined only for sets that are "well behaved" enough.

80
00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:05.480
So how did Vitali build such a monster?

81
00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:09.770
This is the point on which the whole story turns.

82
00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:13.100
Imagine infinitely many boxes.

83
00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.950
Inside each box there is at least one ball.

84
00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:20.560
You are asked to choose one ball from each box and put it into a new bag.

85
00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.930
It sounds innocent.

86
00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:28.660
If the boxes are finite in number there is no problem at all; you go one by one and take them.

87
00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:40.950
If the boxes are infinite but you have a rule for making the choice, again there is no problem; you can say, for instance, "take the smallest ball in each box," and the rule makes all the choices at once on your behalf.

88
00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.600
But what if the boxes are infinite and no rule works?

89
00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:52.410
What if the contents of the boxes are arranged in such a way that saying "the smallest," "the leftmost," "the first" has no meaning at all?

90
00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:58.780
Then, in order to claim that this bag exists, you have to add it to mathematics as a separate assumption.

91
00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.430
The name of that assumption is the Axiom of Choice.

92
00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:12.480
Ernst Zermelo formulated it explicitly in nineteen oh four, and from the moment it was published it caused controversy, because it can never show you the object it declares to exist.

93
00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.010
It does not describe the bag to you.

94
00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:19.940
It merely says "such a bag exists," and falls silent.

95
00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:23.910
Vitali's non-measurable set is exactly such a bag.

96
00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:27.240
And so are Banach and Tarski's five pieces.

97
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:34.730
Now let's look at what is going on inside the sphere, because the mechanism itself is even more astonishing than the non-measurability.

98
00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:38.060
The key is hidden in the rotations that turn the sphere.

99
00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:43.790
Choose two rotations; let their axes be different and their angles carefully tuned.

100
00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:50.960
By applying these two rotations and their inverses one after another, you can generate infinitely many different motions.

101
00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:59.410
The critical property is this: none of these sequences of motion, unless they cancel one another out, brings the sphere back to where it started.

102
00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:03.700
Every different sequence genuinely gives a different result.

103
00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:11.670
Felix Hausdorff noticed this structure in nineteen fourteen and built the first paradoxical decomposition on the surface of a sphere;

104
00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:18.520
ten years later Banach and Tarski followed the road he had opened and carried it over to the solid ball.

105
00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:24.890
This family of motions is itself a countable infinity, and it branches out like a hierarchical tree.

106
00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:35.260
Now sort all these sequences into four groups according to their first letter: those beginning with the first rotation, those beginning with its inverse, those beginning with the second rotation,

107
00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:38.270
those beginning with its inverse.

108
00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:40.640
Here is where the magic lies.

109
00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:50.370
If you take the group "those beginning with the inverse of the first rotation" and apply the first rotation to every one of them, that inverse move at the front cancels out and falls away.

110
00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:56.740
The pile that remains covers everything that does not begin with the first rotation.

111
00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:02.310
That is, by rotating a single group you have single-handedly rebuilt a large portion of the tree.

112
00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:08.200
Two of the four groups in your hands give you the whole, and the other two give you the whole as well.

113
00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.730
One becomes two.

114
00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:14.380
This is the strangeness of infinity that we already know.

115
00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:22.190
But what Banach and Tarski did was to take that strangeness out of the world of abstract motions and transfer it to the points of a solid body.

116
00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:27.760
They matched every point on the sphere with the family of motion sequences that could be applied to it.

117
00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.410
Then they chose a single representative point from each family.

118
00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:36.660
From countless families, with no rule, infinitely many choices.

119
00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:40.150
And this is where the bag comes in.

120
00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:44.280
And this is why you can never see those five pieces.

121
00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.810
They are not slices.

122
00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:50.940
Not solid shells, not wedges, not cubes.

123
00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:59.390
They are sets of points strewn with infinite fineness across every part of the sphere, with no unbroken surface anywhere, rather like a cloud of dust.

124
00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:04.320
So scattered that the question "how much space does this piece occupy" has no answer.

125
00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:10.210
It isn't that volume fails to be conserved; there was never a volume there to conserve.

126
00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:19.140
When you meet a result like this, the first question that comes to mind is: since all the trouble comes out of that axiom, let's just throw it away.

127
00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:23.910
This is a serious proposal, and it was taken seriously.

128
00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:31.240
In nineteen seventy Robert Solovay built a mathematical universe in which the full strength of the Axiom of Choice is given up.

129
00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:35.210
In that universe every subset of the number line is measurable.

130
00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:38.060
Vitali's monster is gone.

131
00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:41.550
The Banach-Tarski decomposition is gone.

132
00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.880
Everything is well behaved.

133
00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.570
But there is a price, and a heavy one.

134
00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:54.580
The Axiom of Choice is not an ornament standing in some odd corner of mathematics; it is a part welded onto the body.

135
00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:59.510
Take it out and you can no longer say that every vector space has a basis.

136
00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:05.080
The Hahn-Banach theorem, one of the cornerstones of functional analysis, is shaken.

137
00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:13.050
Dozens of results used daily in topology, in algebra, in analysis either collapse or are pulled back to far weaker forms.

138
00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:22.940
It is possible to work inside the universe Solovay built, but there you have to reconstruct a great part of mathematics all over again, by far more laborious routes.

139
00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:27.390
The overwhelming majority of mathematicians chose not to make that trade.

140
00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:32.800
They preferred living with a counterintuitive theorem to abandoning half of the tools that work.

141
00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:37.730
Incidentally, why the paradox is so selective is interesting in its own right.

142
00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:41.380
Banach-Tarski works in at least three dimensions.

143
00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:45.030
It does not work on the number line or in the plane.

144
00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:50.440
You cannot cut a square and make two identical squares; you cannot double a circle.

145
00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:56.970
The reason lies not in dimension itself but in the structure of the motions available in that dimension.

146
00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:06.380
In the plane, rotations and translations behave too compatibly with one another; you cannot build that freely branching tree of motions I just described.

147
00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:14.030
In nineteen twenty-nine John von Neumann analyzed precisely what creates this difference, and gave this property of groups a name.

148
00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:22.320
In the third dimension, though, the axes of rotation multiply, the motions break free of one another, and the tree opens out.

149
00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.130
The door of the paradox swings ajar right there.

150
00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:29.780
Even so, the plane is not entirely innocent.

151
00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:39.030
In nineteen twenty-five Tarski asked a question: can you cut a disc into finitely many pieces and, by sliding the pieces only, make a square of the same area?

152
00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:45.560
This asks not for volume to be created but for shape to be transformed; the area is conserved.

153
00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:49.370
The question stayed open for sixty-five years.

154
00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:54.140
In nineteen ninety Miklós Laczkovich proved that the answer is yes.

155
00:13:54.140 --> 00:14:03.710
The number of pieces was somewhere around ten to the fiftieth power, and the proof again rested on the Axiom of Choice, which means the pieces were once again invisible.

156
00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:13.760
Then in two thousand seventeen Andrew Marks and Spencer Unger went considerably further: they showed that the same transformation can be carried out with describable, constructible pieces.

157
00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:21.730
This time the number of pieces climbed to ten to the two hundredth power, but in exchange the pieces no longer came out of an abstract bag.

158
00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:24.580
They were objects that could be imagined.

159
00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:27.750
Let's come back to the orange on the table.

160
00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:30.440
You can peel it in perfect safety.

161
00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:34.890
Banach-Tarski cannot duplicate your orange, and never will.

162
00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:38.860
Because a real orange is not made of infinitely many points.

163
00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:42.350
It is made of finitely many atoms.

164
00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:52.080
Inside a kilo of orange there is a very large but finite number of particles, and you cannot divide a finite object into finite pieces and double it.

165
00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:56.370
What holds the theorem up is a property not of matter but of continuity.

166
00:14:56.370 --> 00:15:03.060
The assumption that at every point of space there is another point, and inside that one, points at infinite density.

167
00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:08.470
That assumption is a property of the mathematical model, not of the physical universe.

168
00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:11.480
And the real legacy lies right here.

169
00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:15.930
Banach-Tarski is not a calculational trick or an entertaining oddity.

170
00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:18.300
It is a boundary marker.

171
00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:24.670
It shows where the tools mathematics has built in order to describe reality become a law unto themselves.

172
00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:34.400
The notion of continuous space models the physical world with extraordinary success; bridges stand because of it, satellites stay in orbit because of it.

173
00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:40.450
But inside that model, objects with no physical counterpart whatsoever can also make their home.

174
00:15:40.450 --> 00:15:43.940
The model is wider than the thing it describes.

175
00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:47.750
You cannot cut one orange and make two oranges.

176
00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:52.680
But you can cut the thing mathematics calls an orange and make two of them.

177
00:15:52.680 --> 00:16:02.890
The difference in that sentence shaped an entire century of measure theory, of set theory, and of the computational tools that reach today from probability all the way to quantum mechanics.

178
00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:08.780
The place where our intuition failed us turned out to be one of the most fertile grounds in mathematics.

179
00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:12.430
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

180
00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:16.240
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
